<https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description> "\n To illustrate with examples the creation of a schema of RDF properties\n and classes, but using abstract things for people, rather than strings, etc.\n If you have an \"Assistant's phone number\", then really you have an assitant who has\n a phone number - and that is much more useful in general.\n For example, when data about you and your assietnt in merged,\n you will end up with an \"assistant's email\" and the like.\n" <https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> .
<https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description> "$Id: contact.rdf,v 1.18 2011/02/01 04:13:21 timbl Exp $" <https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> .
<https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title> "Contact: Utility concepts for everyday life" <https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> .
<https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/doc#creator> <http://www.w3.org/People/Berners-Lee/card#i> <https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact> .
